Clarity 6: a public or read-only function may be defined under a reserved name that was free in an earlier Clarity version, but only to implement a matching method of an implemented trait deployed while the name was still free. This keeps legacy traits whose method names later became natives (e.g. `slice?`, `stacks-block-height`) implementable at the latest Clarity version; a bare reference inside the contract still resolves to the native, and the function is reachable only by literal-name lookup (`contract-call?` and trait dispatch). An unmatched definition fails with the same `NameAlreadyUsed` error as in earlier versions.
Clarity 6: `define-trait` now rejects method names that are currently reserved; only traits deployed before a name's reservation can make it implementable.
